import Foundation

public enum Longform {
    /// Original coherent synthetic narrative under the repository license.
    /// Held out of the preliminary voice bracket; never repeated to pad its length.
    public static let passage = Passage(id: "heldout-longform", role: "longform", text: """
    The room above the bakery

    When the library roof began to leak, the first person to notice was not the librarian. It was a child named Ben, who had been looking for a book about the moon. A drop landed on the picture of a crater. He looked up, looked back at the page, and held the book sideways, as though the water might decide to go somewhere else. Then he went to find someone who knew what to do.

    Nora had worked at the library for eleven years. She knew which window stuck in winter, which radiator clicked before it grew warm, and which shelf could not take another heavy dictionary. She did not know how to repair a roof. What she knew, in that moment, was that water moved faster than committees. She put a bucket beneath the stain, asked Ben to fetch a second bucket, and started moving the books.

    By closing time, a line of empty spaces ran along the north wall. The books stood in uneven towers on the reading tables. Someone had covered the catalogue computer with a coat. The rain had stopped, but every few minutes another drop fell into the first bucket, making a small, determined sound. Nora stayed until the builder arrived. He climbed into the roof, came down again, and explained that the problem was larger than a loose tile.

    The building would have to close for several months. He said this gently, which somehow made it harder to hear. Nora asked whether a corner could remain open. He shook his head. She asked whether the entrance hall would be safe for collecting reserved books. He shook his head again. Finally she asked for a written description of the work. That, at least, was something she could take to the council meeting on Thursday.

    On the walk home, she passed the bakery. Its windows were bright, and a man was washing flour from the front step. Above the shop were three tall windows with faded green curtains. Nora had never thought about the room behind them. She had walked under those windows almost every day for eleven years. Now she stopped in the rain and looked up until the man with the bucket asked if she had lost something.

    The room had once been used for dance lessons. After that it held boxes, then nothing much at all. There was a staircase at the side of the shop and a small lift that had carried sacks of flour before the bakery changed its deliveries. The owner, Mrs. Patel, found the key in a drawer beneath the till. She did not promise anything. She said they could look, and looking seemed better than standing outside.

    Upstairs, their footsteps sounded unexpectedly loud. There were marks on the floor where the piano had stood, and a narrow balcony overlooking the yard. The room smelled of dust and warm bread. Nora walked from one end to the other, counting paces. It was much smaller than the library. There would be no separate children's room, no quiet study area, and certainly no space for every book. But there was a roof, and it was dry.

    A temporary address

    The council approved a temporary move on the condition that access, fire safety, insurance and costs were checked first. Nora wrote those four headings on a sheet of paper. Under each one she put the name of a person to call. It was reassuring to have a list. It was less reassuring when every call seemed to produce two more questions. Could the lift carry a wheelchair? Who held the inspection record? Did the back stairs lead directly to the street?

    A volunteer called Martin offered to measure the room. He arrived with a tape, a pencil and the confidence of someone who had once built a kitchen. Halfway through the morning he admitted that a public reading room was different from a kitchen. Nora liked him better for saying it. They asked a qualified surveyor to check the measurements. She found that one door needed to be altered, and explained exactly why a few centimetres mattered.

    There was a difficult conversation about money. The repair budget paid for the old roof; it did not automatically pay for a temporary library. Some councillors thought readers could use the branch in the next town. Nora described the bus timetable, the school day, and the distance between the bus stop and that branch. She brought numbers, but she also brought a map. It was the map that made the room grow quiet.

    Mrs. Patel offered the upstairs space at a modest rent. She would not accept the suggestion that she should lend it for nothing. A proper agreement, she said, made things clear for everyone. Nora understood. They wrote down who would pay for heating, cleaning, signs and the door alteration. They recorded the opening hours and the notice required to end the arrangement. Kindness was useful, but clear terms would help their kindness survive a difficult month.

    The library could take roughly a quarter of its collection. Choosing that quarter was harder than Nora expected. Popular books were obvious candidates, yet a shelf filled only with popular books would leave many readers with nothing they wanted. She asked each regular visitor to name one book that should travel and one that could stay in storage. The answers overlapped less than she had imagined. Someone asked for railway maps. Someone else wanted a dictionary of birds.

    Ben chose a large book about the moon, although not the copy with the water mark. He was very clear about that. A teenager named Alice chose a battered collection of plays. Mr. Chen asked for a gardening guide he had borrowed every spring for twenty years. Nora wondered why he had never bought it. He said that buying a thing and being able to find it when needed were not quite the same. She wrote that down after he left.

    Moving day

    They moved on a Monday, when both the bakery and the library were normally closed. Every box had a number. Every number appeared on a list. The system worked perfectly until a volunteer put the list inside Box Twelve. For twenty minutes, nobody could explain why the reference books seemed to have vanished. Then a small girl asked whether the papers in the open box were important. Nora thanked her and attached the list to a clipboard with a bright red string.

    The shelves had to be assembled upstairs because the staircase was too narrow for them. Martin sorted the screws into saucers borrowed from the bakery. He said he would remember which screws belonged where. Mrs. Patel gave him a marker and told him to label the saucers anyway. By lunchtime he had thanked her twice. The last shelf stood beside the window, where the old curtains made a wavering green shadow across the floor.

    Downstairs, the bakers prepared sandwiches. Upstairs, people disagreed about the position of the children's table. Near the window, it would have good light. Near the door, parents could keep an eye on their children while collecting books. In the centre, everyone would trip over its chairs. They tried two arrangements before accepting a third that nobody had suggested at first. Nora noticed that the argument became easier once they actually moved the table instead of pointing at it.

    At four o'clock, a delivery van brought six boxes that should have gone to storage. At half past four, the storage company called to ask why it had received the library's new entrance sign. The mistake was inconvenient rather than disastrous. That distinction became important as the afternoon continued. Nora wrote down what had happened, called both drivers, and asked the volunteers to stop guessing where the missing items might be. Guessing made the room louder without making the boxes arrive.

    When the last volunteer left, Nora sat in a borrowed chair and listened. She could hear a door shutting in the yard, a motor starting somewhere below, and the low hum of the new lights. The room did not sound like the library. It did not look like the library either. Then she saw the gardening guide on its shelf, a blue spine between two green ones, and felt that the place had acquired at least one familiar landmark.

    Mrs. Patel came upstairs with two cups of tea. She asked whether Nora was disappointed. Nora took some time to answer. She had spent weeks saying that the move would be possible. Admitting that it might still be disappointing felt disloyal to everyone who had helped. Finally she said that she missed the old room. Mrs. Patel nodded. Missing one place, she said, did not prevent a person from making good use of another.

    The first week

    On opening morning, the sign pointed toward the bakery counter instead of the staircase. Three people asked for library cards while Mrs. Patel was serving bread. One person asked Nora whether the upstairs room sold coffee. By ten o'clock the sign had been moved, and another sign explained that the bakery and library kept separate tills. Nora resisted adding a third sign about reading the first two. People were learning a new address, and so was she.

    The lift worked, but it was slower than the stairs. A reader using a walking frame said that being slow was not the same as being inconvenient, provided nobody stood outside looking impatient. Nora moved a chair away from the lift entrance to give people more room. She also asked the volunteers not to offer help before it was needed. The reader returned the next afternoon and spent an hour choosing a book. That felt like a useful answer.

    The room was busiest after school. Children came in with their coats open and their voices still at playground volume. Adults who had hoped for silence looked up from the newspapers. Nora explained the space to both groups. She did not ask the children to disappear, and she did not tell the adults that quiet no longer mattered. They established an hour for homework, a corner for conversation, and a few rules that could fit on one sheet of paper.

    Alice began helping with the younger readers on Wednesdays. She had expected to read aloud. Instead, the first child asked her to listen while he read to her. He stopped often and started sentences again. Alice waited. Later she told Nora that waiting had been much harder than reading the story herself. Nora said the child had probably noticed her patience even if he could not have named it. The next week he brought a longer book.

    Not everything worked. The newspapers arrived late, the borrowed chairs were uncomfortable, and the room became warm whenever the ovens ran at full capacity. Someone complained that the smell of bread made it impossible to concentrate. Someone else said it was the first library that made them feel hungry in a pleasant way. Nora entered both comments in the same notebook. She would not improve the room by treating praise as evidence and complaints as noise.

    On Friday, rain began just before closing. The readers nearest the windows paused and looked at the ceiling. Nora looked too. No stain appeared. The gutters carried the water into the yard, and the tall windows reflected the lamps above the tables. Ben stayed until his mother called from the stairs. Before leaving, he asked Nora whether the moon book could come home with him. This time he carried it flat, safely tucked beneath his coat.

    The question of returning

    The roof repairs took longer than the builder first estimated. Hidden timber damage required a new plan. A supplier missed a delivery. Then a week of high winds made work on the scaffolding unsafe. Each delay had a clear explanation, but their combined effect was discouraging. Nora updated the notice by the door whenever she had reliable information. When she had no new information, she said that plainly. She refused to invent a date simply because an empty space looked untidy.

    Meanwhile, the temporary room developed habits of its own. Mr. Chen brought a small pot of mint for the window. The bakers saved the clean sides of large paper sheets for children's drawings. Alice started a noticeboard for local performances. Someone made a map showing the books that remained in storage and how to request them. The room grew more useful, but also more complicated. Every addition occupied space that another person might have used differently.

    At a staff meeting, a volunteer suggested staying above the bakery permanently. Several people agreed. Others looked uncomfortable. Nora asked them to describe what they would gain and what they would lose. The bakery room was central, friendly and inexpensive. It was also small, dependent on a lift, and unsuitable for parts of the collection. Liking a place did not answer every question about how it should be used. They made two lists instead of taking an immediate vote.

    That evening, Nora visited the old building. The tables had been covered, and most of the shelves were empty. Through an opening in the ceiling she could see new timber beside the old. The builder pointed out the sections that had been repaired. He did not describe the damage as a fortunate opportunity. It had been costly and disruptive. But he showed her a change to the drainage that would make the same problem less likely to return.

    Standing in the silent reading room, Nora remembered the arguments about the temporary children's table. There was more space here, yet they had allowed the old arrangement to become a kind of rule. Nobody had moved the tables for years. She measured the distance from the entrance to the large window and wondered whether the familiar room could work differently. Returning, she realised, did not have to mean pretending that the interruption had taught them nothing.

    She took photographs, without readers or personal papers in them, and brought them to the next meeting. On the photographs she placed paper shapes representing tables and shelves. People could move those shapes instead of merely defending an idea. The discussion still became heated when they reached the newspaper corner. Then Mr. Chen shifted a paper rectangle by a few centimetres. Someone laughed, and they began looking for a practical arrangement rather than a winning argument.

    A place for the missing books

    Requests from storage revealed something else. Readers sometimes wanted books that had scarcely been borrowed before the move. A local history collection attracted new attention because its titles appeared on the request map. An atlas that had lived behind a pillar was suddenly in demand. The books had not changed. Their visibility had. Nora wondered how many apparent preferences were really consequences of what people could easily find.

    She compared the request list with the old shelf layout. She did not conclude that every quiet corner had been a mistake. Some readers valued the chance to browse without being watched. Others needed clear labels and an unobstructed route. A useful library had to support both. Nora invited a few visitors to walk through the proposed arrangement on paper. She asked them where they would begin, and listened when their answers surprised her.

    One visitor could not read the small shelf labels even with glasses. Another found the colours on the new map difficult to distinguish. These were not problems that better instructions would solve. Nora enlarged the labels, added words beside the colours, and checked the routes again. She kept the earlier versions in a folder, with notes explaining what had changed. Otherwise someone might later restore an attractive design without knowing why it had been replaced.

    Ben contributed a drawing of the moon, with an arrow pointing toward the science shelves. Nora thanked him but explained that the sign would need words as well. He returned the next day with the word ‘Science’ written beneath the drawing in large, careful letters. The arrow now pointed in the wrong direction. They turned it together. It became the first sign approved for the returning collection, although neither of them used such formal language at the time.

    Mrs. Patel watched the preparations with mixed feelings. She would be glad to have access to the upstairs room again. She would miss the regular visitors, the questions, and the sound of children discovering that a book could contain a joke. She said this without suggesting that gratitude required the library to stay. Nora appreciated that. A temporary agreement could have a good ending without becoming a permanent arrangement.

    They decided to hold one final evening above the bakery. There would be no long speeches. Anyone who wished could read a short passage from a favourite book or explain why a particular title mattered to them. For people who did not want to speak, there would be cards to write on. Alice asked whether a performance had to be polished. Nora said that it had to be short enough to leave time for the next person. Everything else could be negotiated.

    The last evening upstairs

    The room filled before the scheduled start. Some people stood at the back; others borrowed chairs from the bakery. Nora kept the route to the lift clear. Martin opened with a story about the lost clipboard, although everyone who had moved the books insisted that his version left out an important detail. He offered them the microphone. None accepted. The story became longer anyway, and Nora quietly raised the card that meant his time was nearly over.

    Alice read part of a play with two friends. They had chosen a scene in which nobody agreed about what had just happened. At first the audience laughed at the characters. Then, perhaps recognising something familiar, they laughed a little more softly. Mr. Chen did not read from his gardening guide. He described the year he had planted peas too early, and the neighbour who had given him enough seedlings to start again. He finished before Nora needed the card.

    Ben had planned to explain the phases of the moon. When his turn arrived, he looked at the faces in the room and could not remember the first sentence. His mother began to help, but Alice held up a hand. They waited. Ben looked down at his drawing, took a breath, and said that the moon did not produce its own light. After that, the rest came more easily. Nobody corrected the small mistake he made near the end.

    Nora spoke last. She thanked the people who had done visible work and the people whose work had mostly gone unnoticed. She named the cleaner who came early, the driver who returned after his shift, and the surveyor who had explained the door. She did not say that the leak had brought them together for a reason. They could have come together without a damaged roof. What mattered was that, when the problem arrived, people had chosen to help.

    After the chairs were stacked, Mrs. Patel opened the balcony door. The yard was dark except for a light over the loading entrance. Nora could hear someone locking a bicycle in the street. They stood without speaking for a while. Downstairs, the night baker began preparing the next day's dough. The room would soon be empty, but the building would continue its ordinary work. Nora found that reassuring. An ending did not require the world to stop around it.

    Home, with changes

    The return move was better organised, though not perfect. The numbered boxes arrived in order. The clipboard remained attached to Nora's wrist. Two shelves had been placed too close together, and a delivery of new books appeared just when the entrance was busiest. They made room, checked the list and continued. This time, when someone proposed a solution, another person asked whether they could test it before committing to it. The question saved a surprising amount of effort.

    On the first morning back, regular visitors paused inside the door. The room was familiar, but the tables had moved. The labels were larger. The newspaper corner had better light, and the science shelves carried a small drawing of the moon. Some people approved immediately. Others said they preferred the old arrangement. Nora invited them to try the new one for a fortnight and tell her which parts caused trouble. She did not ask them to promise that they would like it.

    Ben found his book without help. Mr. Chen returned the gardening guide and borrowed it again a few minutes later. Alice inspected the noticeboard, moved one poster slightly to the left, and sat down with a play she had not read. Across the room, a child sounded out the first line of a story while an adult waited beside him. Nora listened to the pause before the next word. It was not an empty pause. Someone was doing work inside it.

    At closing time, she checked the windows and switched off the reading lamps. On the north wall, the repair had left a rectangle of fresh paint that did not quite match the older surface. The builder had offered to repaint the whole wall. Nora would arrange it eventually. For now, the difference reminded her where the books had stood when the first drop fell. She emptied the unused bucket, put it in the cupboard, and turned its handle neatly toward the door.

    Walking home, she passed the bakery. The upstairs curtains were open. For a moment she could see the bare floor and the pale marks where the temporary shelves had been. Then Mrs. Patel appeared at the window and waved. Nora waved back. She had not stopped missing that room simply because she was glad to have the library again. The two feelings fitted together better than she had expected. She continued down the street as the bakery lights came on behind her.
    """)
}
